+The results show that several machine-learning and deep-learning models achieved similar levels of performance for short-term regional precipitation forecasting over Dublin. Across five fixed random seeds, BiLSTM-Attention obtained the lowest mean RMSE of 0.2429~mm\,h$^{-1}$, followed closely by XGBoost at 0.2439~mm\,h$^{-1}$. The difference between the two models was small relative to the overall forecast error. The paired block-bootstrap analysis showed no clear RMSE difference for the complete test set. The legacy wet-subset interval is retained only for its original definition. For the revised wet subset ($\geq0.1$~mm\,h$^{-1}$), the separate single-saved-run comparison supports lower RMSE for XGBoost, as reported in Section~\ref{sec:overall_results}; it is not a five-seed comparison. The main result is therefore not that one model consistently outperformed all alternatives, but that a relatively compact recurrent model and a tree-based machine-learning model produced comparable overall point forecasts under the same data and evaluation protocol. \revminor{The comparable overall performance of the tree-based and recurrent models is consistent with broader evidence that tree-based methods remain competitive with deep neural networks on medium-sized tabular datasets \citep{grinsztajn2022tree}. Related precipitation-focused work has also shown that shallower U-Net configurations can achieve performance comparable to deeper variants with fewer trainable parameters \citep{otero2023intercomparison}. Although these studies differ in data representation, architecture and prediction setting, they reinforce the importance of evaluating model complexity against demonstrated predictive gains rather than assuming that more complex models will necessarily perform better.}
 \par}
 
 \revminor{The comparison between BiLSTM-Attention and the otherwise identical BiLSTM-NoAttention control isolates the temporal attention layer. Attention reduced mean RMSE from 0.2447 to 0.2429~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (paired difference $-0.0018$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, $-0.73\%$); all five seeds improved, and both the seed-t and moving-block intervals, each $[-0.0033,-0.0003]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, excluded zero. After Holm adjustment over the 16 all-target RMSE comparisons in Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3sig_pairs}, however, the adjusted $p$ values were 0.17 (seed-t) and 0.13 (block), and the wet and upper-tail differences were not clearly separated. The attention layer added 8,320 trainable parameters (36,993 to 45,313). The gain is therefore consistent in sign but small, and it is not established after multiplicity adjustment.}
